/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  filter04_leafFilter_sameAsFiltrateWash

  A BATCH LEAF FILTER WASHED ALONG THE FILTRATE'S PATH.  filter02's press,
  slurry and cycle (Geankoplis, Hersel & Lepek 5th ed., Examples 24.2-1 to
  24.2-3: alpha = 1.863e11 m/kg, R_m = 10.63e10 1/m, 338 kPa, 17.46 m2,
  3.37 m3 of filtrate per cycle, 20 min to clean, wash water 10 % of the
  filtrate), with ONE change: `washFlowPath sameAsFiltrate`.  The wash
  follows the filtrate's own path, as in a leaf filter, so it runs at the
  FINAL FILTRATION RATE, 1/(K_p V_f + B) (eq. 24.2-19), and not at the
  quarter of it a plate-and-frame press's through-washing gets (eq.
  24.2-20, filter02).  That branch had no case.

  HELD TO (the hand-written rows at the top of `expected`): K_p, B, the
  filtration time, the wash time, the cycle time and the presses needed,
  recomputed by an independent script (verification/leafClosedForm.py,
  Python standard library, written from Geankoplis's equations, not from
  the C++), with the water's viscosity and density the package publishes
  as its inputs.  The wash time is one quarter of filter02's on the same
  inputs, which the same script prints.

  WHAT ITS NUMBERS ARE NOT: a validation, and not one of Geankoplis's
  answers -- his Example 24.2-3 washes THROUGH the press.  The feed is
  still the one sized for filter02's longer cycle, so fewer than one press
  passes it here (`pressesRequired` < 1), which is a consequence of the
  change, not a design.  Water washes water (the sealed records carry no
  solute), with the `displacement` law.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
application   choupoSolve;
description   "A batch leaf filter washed along the filtrate's path (washFlowPath sameAsFiltrate, Geankoplis eq. 24.2-19) on filter02's CaCO3 slurry and press: the wash at the final filtration rate, held to an independent recomputation.";
verbosity     3;
citations
(
    "Geankoplis, C.J.; Hersel, A.A.; Lepek, D.H. Transport Processes and Separation Process Principles, 5th ed. Prentice Hall (year not read on the page). Section 24.2, eqs. 24.2-13 to 24.2-20, Examples 24.2-1 to 24.2-4, pp. 1078-1090."
);
